The EasyList-family dual licence — which branch Ziy relies on
--------------------------------------------------------------

EasyList, EasyPrivacy and the Adblock Warning Removal List are offered by
their maintainers under a dual grant: the GNU General Public License version
3, or the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported licence
(CC-BY-SA 3.0). The upstream licence page describes the lists as "dual
licensed under the GNU General Public License version 3 … and Creative
Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported", with "The EasyList authors"
attributed as the source — https://easylist.to/pages/licence.html.

Ziy ships the verbatim list bodies (above) with attribution and a link to
the upstream licence, which satisfies either branch of the grant. Where a
single branch must be chosen — for the compiled ruleset that ships in the xpi
(`rules/static-ruleset.json`, written by `tools/gen-static-ruleset.js`) — Ziy
relies on the CC-BY-SA 3.0 branch. No Ziy authorship is claimed over the
ruleset: it is a mechanical compilation of the lists above, and this file
(which ships inside the same xpi) is the attribution that travels with it.


What the extension does with these files
----------------------------------------

At build time, `tools/gen-static-ruleset.js` parses the snapshots, compiles the
network rules to declarativeNetRequest rules, and writes
`extensions/ziy-shields/rules/static-ruleset.json`, which is what ships in the
xpi. The snapshots themselves also ship, because they are the input to the
update mechanism and to the offline first-run.

At runtime, the list registry (`lib/filterlist/lists.js`) records, per list, the
URL it came from, the licence it declares, and a SHA-256 of the body it
accepted. Every list toggle and every custom list the user adds is recorded the
same way, so a user can always answer "what is this rule from, and who wrote it".

The licence and homepage of each shipped list are shown in the options page's
Filter lists section, next to its rule count and checksum.


Design note — what is NOT here
------------------------------

There is no allow-list of major sites. The behaviour that provides — sites that
must not be broken by blocking — comes from the exception rules (`@@`) that
these lists carry themselves, and from `first_party_protections`, which the
compiler handles as the `LIST_EXCEPTION` priority tier. Ziy does not maintain,
ship or invent a separate list of "do not break these" domains, because the
correct behaviour is already expressible as the lists' own exceptions and
inventing a second, weaker version of it would be worse than using the real one.
